00:03:21.411 --> 00:03:23.540
The first real question I want to pose is,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.940
How do we actually define good animation?

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.140
Which sounds like a stupid obvious question, but stick with me here.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.980
Unlike graphics and video games

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.060
Or CGI and movies

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.260
There is not one pinnacle that we are heading towards and can set a benchmark against.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:40.440
We are not aiming for true immersion with photorealistic details.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.560
I mean, this is animation.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.780
The whole point of animation is that it is NOT real.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.580
Sure most of the time we can collectively agree, which animation looks better.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.240
Like if you watch an average show,

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.160
Let's say, 'All You Need is a Sister'
[It's actually, 'A Sister's All You Need' but whatever]

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:53.880
And you're like,

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:54.414
"Alright.

00:03:54.414 --> 00:03:55.720
Okay, this is adequate.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.340
It's not blowing my mind, but it's definitely doing the job it needs to do."

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.360
And then you pop in 'Red Line' and it's just like,

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:02.400
"Wow 'Red Line'.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.360
Why not buy my eyes dinner first before trying to FuCK them...

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.120
Please."

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:07.940
"Ooh, 'Red Line'!

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:08.440
Okay.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.560
Well my eyes don't normally put down a first date, but God you just look so good right now!

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.140
Okay, well now you're just pushing too hard and now I'm gonna have to file a sexual assault claim."

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:20.460
We can break it down to more frames of animation or more movement and fluidity on screen,

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.940
But honestly who's to say that the sensation overload of 'Red Line' has,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.380
"GOODER ANIMATION" (-Gigguk 2017)

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.220
Than the ethereal photo realistic art style of Makoto Shinkai's works

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.740
Or the detailed expresive character animation in Yamada's "A Silent Voice"

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.560
When we get to animation of this level,

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:38.100
Becomes less a concrete metric we can judge against,

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.500
And more the personal bias and interpretation of the viewer watching it.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:42.600
Going back to video games,

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.120
We can look at some graphics and think,

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.760
"Wow I almost can't tell the difference between this and real-life!

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.240
This is jaw-dropping!"

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.160
And in anime, you can look at a cut and say,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.040
"Wow! This is so smooth and realistic!

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:55.520
THIS is jaw-dropping!"

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:01.640
But is this more impressive than the way Masaaki Yuasa warps and distorts to convey an idea with his surrealism?

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:05.560
Or in Imaishi's in-your-face-louder-than-life-hard-hitting cuts

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.820
Which is the animation equivalent of twenty Sylvester Stallone's kicking you in the nutsack simultaneously?

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:13.899
And I know there's more thoughts put into graphics in video games and realism,

00:05:13.899 --> 00:05:16.240
But please don't make this discussion about that.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:17.409
I was trying to make a point.

00:05:17.409 --> 00:05:20.580
I think it's important to note that something can be technically well animated,

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:22.900
But still look like SHIT for other reasons

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:26.480
And I don't think there is a better example of this than the Naruto-Pain fight

